.TH WAYHUD 1 "2026-09-01" "wayhud" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wayhud \- layer\-shell text overlay for sway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wayhud
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[\fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wayhud
puts a message on screen above every window, as a Wayland layer\-shell
overlay on the
.B overlay
layer. The text can appear all at once or be typed out terminal\-style with
a caret and a synthesised blip per keystroke, and it leaves with one of
several exit animations.
.PP
It is a one\-shot program: it shows the message, waits, and exits. Two
concurrent invocations are two processes and two layer surfaces, which the
compositor stacks.
.PP
The overlay keeps an empty input region, so it never intercepts pointer or
keyboard events \- whatever is underneath stays usable.
.PP
.I TEXT
is taken from the command line; if it is omitted or given as
.BR - ,
the message is read from standard input instead. Either way the message is
limited to 100000 characters: everything downstream scales with the text, so
an unbounded pipe would exhaust memory before the first frame. Reading stops
at that limit rather than at end of file, so a stream that never ends \-
.B journalctl \-f
and the like \- is refused with an error instead of growing until the machine
gives out. Nothing is drawn until the input is complete. In a command\-line
argument the escapes
.BR \en ,
.B \et
and
.B \e\e
are expanded, because sway runs
.B exec
through
.BR sh ,
which has no
.BR $'...'
quoting. Text arriving on standard input is used verbatim.
.PP
Trailing newlines are dropped whichever way the text arrives. Pango turns one
into an empty final line and counts it in the height, so the surface comes out
a line taller than the message: the compositor centres that phantom line along
with the text, and everything sits half a line off.
.SH OPTIONS
Flags that stand for a config table \-
.BR \-\-outline ,
.BR \-\-glow ,
.BR \-\-position ,
.BR \-\-reveal ,
.BR \-\-vanish ,
.B \-\-sound
\- all take the same
.IR SPEC :
an optional bare value first, then comma\-separated
.IR key = value
pairs. The keys are spelled exactly as the TOML keys are, so
.B \-\-glow \(aq#b8bb26,radius=12\(aq
and
.B glow = { color = \(dq#b8bb26\(dq, radius = 12 }
are the same words in the same order.
.PP
The bare value is the field the flag is usually about \- the colour for
.B \-\-glow
and
.BR \-\-outline ,
the kind for
.B \-\-reveal
and
.BR \-\-vanish ,
.BR on / off
for
.BR \-\-sound .
It may also be written by name; giving it both ways is an error rather than
something to resolve.
.PP
A field the spec does not mention keeps the preset's value, which is what makes
these composable:
.B \-\-glow \(aqalpha=0.3\(aq
dims a halo the preset already configured without restating its colour or
radius. An unknown field is refused with the list of the ones that exist, the
way an unknown key is refused in the config file. Ranges are checked by the
same code the config file goes through, so a bound cannot hold in one place
and not the other.
.PP
Commas inside brackets are left alone, so a CSS colour such as
.B rgb(184, 187, 38)
is one field rather than three.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fISPEC\fR"
Which outputs to show on:
.B current
(the output sway currently has focus on),
.B all
(every enabled output), or a comma\-separated list of connector names such as
.BR DP\-3,eDP\-1 .
Default:
.BR current .
.IP
.B current
queries sway over its IPC socket, because Wayland gives a client no way to
learn which output is focused. The socket is located the way
.BR swaymsg (1)
does it:
.BR I3SOCK ,
then
.BR SWAYSOCK ,
then asking
.B i3
or
.B sway
for its path. If none of those work it fails rather than guessing. A named
output that does not exist is reported and skipped; if nothing matches at
all, that is an error.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fISECONDS\fR"
How long the fully revealed text stays up. Measured from the
.I end
of the reveal, not from start\-up, so a slow typewriter does not eat into
the reading time. Defaults to the selected preset's
.BR timeout_ms ,
which is 5 seconds unless the config says otherwise.
.IP
The whole lifetime \- reveal plus hold plus vanish \- is capped at one hour,
since nothing can dismiss a HUD early.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-style " \fINAME\fR"
Style preset from the config file. Default:
.BR default .
An unknown name is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-font " \fIDESC\fR"
Pango font description, e.g.
.B "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq"
or
.BR "\(dqFiraCode Nerd Font 72\(dq" .
The default is
.BR "\(dqMonospace 72\(dq" ,
a fontconfig generic that resolves to whatever the system has configured for
that role. A named family must be spelled the way fontconfig spells it, which
is rarely how the vendor writes it; one that does not resolve falls back to
the system default without a word of warning. Check a name with
.BR fc\-match (1).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " \fICOLOUR\fR"
Fill colour of the glyphs. Any colour GTK's CSS parser accepts:
.BR #b8bb26 ,
.BR "rgba(184,187,38,0.8)" ,
.BR green .
.TP
.BR \-\-outline " \fISPEC\fR"
Stroke the glyphs, or
.B none
for flat text. Fields:
.BR color ,
.B width
(logical pixels, 0 to 128).
.IP
Real cairo path, not a shadow, so it stays clean at display sizes. Without
.B width
the flag changes only the colour and leaves the stroke as it was \- which,
left unset, scales with the font size, keeping the halo a fixed fraction of
the stem: a constant width that looks like a fine contour at 72pt reads as
fake bold at 24pt. A width on
.B none
is rejected; there is no stroke to size.
.TP
.BR \-\-glow " \fISPEC\fR"
Paint a soft halo behind the glyphs, or
.B none
for no halo. Fields:
.BR color ,
.B radius
(logical pixels, 0 to 128),
.B alpha
(0 to 1).
.IP
The halo is drawn
.I under
the outline rather than instead of it, so the two combine: a dark contour with
a coloured bloom outside it is one setting, not a choice. It is blurred once
per output when the message is built, and it widens the padding \- which comes
out of the wrapping budget, so a very large radius makes long lines wrap
sooner.
.TP
.BR \-\-position " \fISPEC\fR"
Where the text block sits. The bare value is a placement word:
.BR center ,
.BR top ,
.BR bottom ,
.BR left ,
.BR right ,
or a hyphenated pair such as
.B top\-left
or
.BR bottom\-right ,
in either order. It names BOTH axes, so
.B bottom
is bottom\-centre. Spelled
.BR center ,
the way the config file spells it; the
.B centre
this flag took before 1.0 is gone, so that the two cannot drift apart again.
Fields:
.BR halign ,
.BR valign ,
which set one axis each and leave the other alone. A centred axis ignores the
margin.
.TP
.BR \-\-margin " \fIPX\fR"
Gap from the anchored edge, in logical pixels. Measured to the SURFACE, not to
the text: the surface carries transparent padding for the outline, the halo
and the caret. Ignored on a centred axis.
.TP
.BR \-\-line\-align " \fIALIGN\fR"
Alignment of the lines within the text block:
.BR left ,
.B center
or
.BR right .
Distinct from
.BR \-\-position ,
which places the block itself.
.TP
.BR \-\-reveal " \fISPEC\fR"
How the text appears. The bare value is the kind:
.B instant
or
.BR typewriter .
Fields, all belonging to the typewriter:
.B cps
(characters per second, positive),
.B cursor
(true/false),
.B jitter
(0 to 1),
.B scroll
(true/false).
.IP
.B jitter
randomises each gap between keystrokes by up to that fraction of it, either
way, so the typing stops sounding like a metronome; the variation is
symmetric, so the reveal still takes about
.I chars
/
.I cps
seconds. The blips follow the same moments as the glyphs \- they are the same
numbers, not a second calculation \- so sound and animation cannot drift apart.
.IP
.B scroll
is terminal mode: the write head stays on the LAST line of the block and
reaching a newline lifts everything already written by one line, instead of
the block being filled downwards from its top edge. See
.B TERMINAL MODE
below.
.IP
A field over a preset that reveals instantly is an error rather than a flag
that quietly does nothing: there is nothing to adjust, so name the kind and
switch the typewriter on deliberately.
.TP
.BR \-\-vanish " \fISPEC\fR"
How the text goes away. The bare value is the kind:
.BR instant ,
.BR fade ,
.BR collapse ,
.BR wash ,
.BR untype ,
.BR dissolve .
Fields:
.B ms
(duration; not accepted on
.BR instant ),
.B dir
.RB ( up / down ,
only on
.BR wash ).
.RS
.TP
.B instant
Gone on the frame the hold expires.
.TP
.B fade
Alpha to zero.
.TP
.B collapse
CRT power\-off: squashes to a bright line, blooms slightly wider, washes to
white, blinks out.
.TP
.B wash
A soft edge sweeps through the text and erases it as it passes.
.TP
.B untype
The caret walks back and eats the text, blipping on the way out. The only one
that makes noise, because it is typing; it gets a caret even when the reveal
was instant.
.TP
.B dissolve
The text falls apart into blocks, in a fixed pseudo\-random order.
.RE
.IP
Without
.B ms
the flag keeps whatever duration the preset had, so effects can be cycled
through without restating the timing; without a kind,
.B \-\-vanish ms=800
retimes the effect the preset chose.
.TP
.BR \-\-sound " \fISPEC\fR"
The typewriter blip. The bare value is
.B on
or
.BR off .
Fields:
.B enabled
(true/false),
.B freq
(100 to 8000 Hz),
.B decay_ms
(10 to 3000),
.B gain
(0 to 1),
.B every
(blip once every N revealed characters, at least 1).
.IP
Switching it off does not reset the knobs, and a preset that silenced itself
can be switched back on.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Take
.I TEXT
literally: do not expand
.BR \en ,
.B \et
or
.BR \e\e .
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIPATH\fR"
Config file to read. Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH TERMINAL MODE
The typewriter fills a block in one of two ways, and
.B \-\-reveal scroll=true
picks the second.
.PP
At the default the block is filled downwards from its top edge: it is sized for
the finished message up front, so the write head starts at the top and walks
down through the room reserved for the rest. With
.B scroll
set, the write head stays on the LAST line of the block and reaching a newline
lifts everything already written by one line.
.PP
Nothing else differs. The surface is sized from the finished message and placed
before the first character is drawn either way, so the mode moves the text
inside the box and never the box itself \- it needs no particular anchor and
holds under any
.BR \-\-position .
With
.B bottom
the writing sits on the bottom margin the whole way through, which is the
terminal look; with
.B center
or
.B top
the block stays where it was put and fills from its own bottom edge upwards. A
message with no newlines draws identically in both modes; there is nothing
above it to lift.
.PP
An
.B untype
vanish runs the rule backwards, since the offset follows the write head and
not the clock: the block slides back down as the lines are eaten.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B wayhud
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wayhud/config.toml
(falling back to
.IR ~/.config/wayhud/config.toml ).
A missing file is fine; a malformed one is an error, as is an unknown key,
so a typo is reported instead of being silently ignored.
.PP
The file is a flat map of named presets, selected with
.BR \-\-style .
.B [style.default]
is the base for every other preset: a key a preset does not set is taken from
there, and only then from the built\-in default. Command\-line flags override
the selected preset for that invocation.
.PP
Every key, its type and its default are documented in
.BR wayhud (5).
.SH SOUND
The typewriter blip is synthesised at run time rather than loaded from a
file, so the program carries no assets. The knobs match those of
.BR blyamk (1);
a sound dialled in there with
.B blyamk \-v
transfers verbatim into a
.B [style.X.sound]
block.
.PP
The entire click track is mixed before the first frame and written to
PulseAudio in one go, so the clicks stay locked to the characters instead of
inheriting the sound server's per\-write scheduling jitter. If no sound
server is reachable, the message is still shown and the failure is reported
on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
A message on the focused output, default style:
.B wayhud \(dqSYSTEM ONLINE\(dq
.TP
Ten seconds, every screen, pinned to the top:
.B wayhud \-o all \-t 10 \-\-position top \(dqBUILD FAILED\(dq
.TP
Two lines from a keybinding in the sway config:
.B bindsym $mod+Shift+h exec wayhud \(dqLOCKED\enBACK IN 5\(dq
.TP
Piped in, no animation, no sound:
.B echo \(dq$(uptime)\(dq | wayhud \-\-reveal instant \-\-sound off
.TP
Self\-destructing note:
.B wayhud \-s spy \-\-vanish \(aquntype,ms=900\(aq \(dqTHIS MESSAGE WILL SELF DESTRUCT\(dq
.TP
A boot log crawling up the bottom corner:
.B wayhud \-\-reveal \(aqscroll=true\(aq \-\-position bottom\-left \(dqCHECKING DISKS\enMOUNTING /\enOK\(dq
.TP
A quieter, slower blip:
.B wayhud \-\-sound \(aqfreq=1200,gain=0.1,every=2\(aq \(dqSTANDING BY\(dq
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B I3SOCK
Path to the compositor's IPC socket, checked first. Set by
.BR i3 ;
sway honours it too.
.TP
.B SWAYSOCK
Path to sway's IPC socket, checked when
.B I3SOCK
is unset. One of the two, or a working
.BR "sway \-\-get\-socketpath" ,
is required for
.BR "\-\-output current" .
.TP
.B GSK_RENDERER
Not read by
.B wayhud
itself, but worth knowing about. Under GTK's default Vulkan renderer some
compositors make every repaint log
.RB \(dq vkQueuePresentKHR ():
.I A swapchain no longer matches the surface properties exactly
\(dq on standard error. That is
.BR VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR ,
a Vulkan
.I success
code \- the frame was presented, and only GDK's decision to report it as a
warning makes it visible. Setting
.B GSK_RENDERER
to
.B opengl
or
.B cairo
silences it with no visible difference to the overlay. It is not set here,
because choosing a renderer for the whole graphics stack is too large a
decision to make on behalf of a message that says everything worked.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the config file, used only when it holds an
.I absolute
path. Unset, empty or relative, it falls back to
.IR $HOME/.config .
An empty value taken at face value would resolve the config relative to the
current directory, which is how a file in
.I $HOME
goes unread with no complaint.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The message was shown and its lifetime ran out.
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, unreadable or invalid config, no matching output, or no
display.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wayhud (5),
.BR sway (1),
.BR swaymsg (1)
